1	<div align="center">
2	<img src="https://public-astrix-bucket.s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/mcp-wrapper-banner.png" alt="MCP Secret Wrapper Logo" maxWidth="500px"/>
3	
4	[![License: Apache 2.0](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-Apache%202.0-blue.svg)](https://opensource.org/licenses/Apache-2.0)
5	
6	</div>
7	
8	Securely inject secrets from vault systems into MCP (Model Context Protocol) servers without exposing credentials in configuration files.
9	
10	## Usage
11	
12	Convert any MCP Server configured for your client and uses a static secret in 2 simple steps:
13	
14	1. Add the secret to one of the [supported vaults](#support)
15	2. Replace the server's settings in the configuration file to use MCP Secret Wrapper:
16	   - Run MCP Secret Wrapper with `npx`
17	   - Pass any environment variables that should be injected. Their value is the secret location.
18	   - Place the separator (`--`) followed by the previous command and all its arguments
19	   - Add the required `VAULT_TYPE` environment variable and any additional optional environment variables either in the `env` section or as CLI arguments (`--vault-type`). You can find examples of both usages in our [Example MCP Server](./example/README.md)
20	
21	### Example Usage - GitHub MCP with AWS Secret Manager
22	
23	**Before: Config Environment Variable (❌ Insecure - Hardcoded Secret)**
24	
25	```json
26	{
27	  "mcpServers": {
28	    "githubApi": {
29	      "command": "docker",
30	      "args": [
31	        "run",
32	        "-i",
33	        "--rm",
34	        "-e",
35	        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN",
36	        "ghcr.io/github/github-mcp-server"
37	      ],
38	      "env": {
39	        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN": "github_pat_EXAMPLE"
40	      }
41	    }
42	  }
43	}
44	```
45	
46	**After: Using Vault (✅ No Exposed Secret)**
47	
48	```json
49	{
50	  "mcpServers": {
51	    "githubApi": {
52	      "command": "npx",
53	      "args": [
54	        "-y",
55	        "git+https://github.com/astrix-security/mcp-secret-wrapper",
56	        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN=arn:aws:secretsmanager:us-east-1:123456789012:secret:example-ABCDE",
57	        "--",
58	        "docker",
59	        "run",
60	        "-i",
61	        "--rm",
62	        "-e",
63	        "GITHUB_PERSONAL_ACCESS_TOKEN",
64	        "ghcr.io/github/github-mcp-server"
65	      ],
66	      "env": {
67	        "VAULT_TYPE": "aws",
68	        "VAULT_PROFILE": "aws-profile",
69	        "VAULT_REGION": "us-east-1"
70	      }
71	    }
72	  }
73	}
74	```
75	
76	https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/9af6e6b3-3694-49de-b107-8749e0630db3
77	
78	**See what's going on behind-the-scenes by using our example MCP server:** [Example MCP Server](./example/README.md)
79	
80	## JSON Path Extraction
81	
82	When your vault stores secrets as JSON objects, you can extract specific values using dot-notation paths. This is useful when a single secret contains multiple values that need to be injected as separate environment variables.
83	
84	### How It Works
85	
86	Append a `#` delimiter followed by a JSON path to your secret ID. The path uses dot notation to navigate through nested JSON objects.
87	
88	**Format:** `SECRET_ID#path.to.value`
89	
90	### Example: Database Configuration
91	
92	Suppose your vault contains a secret with the following JSON structure:
93	
94	```json
95	{
96	  "db": {
97	    "credentials": {
98	      "username": "demo_user",
99	      "password": "demo_password"
100	    },

